NAME
       ldd - print shared object dependencies

SYNOPSIS
       ldd FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       Print the shared objects (shared libraries) that each FILE needs at run
       time, in load order, as

           NAME => PATH (0xBASE)

       or "NAME => not found" when a dependency cannot be located. The exit
       status is 1 if any dependency is missing or a FILE cannot be examined.

       For a dynamically linked executable ldd starts it with the environment
       variable LD_TRACE_LOADED_OBJECTS=1. The dynamic loader
       (/lib/ld-purec.so.1) then maps the dependencies, prints them and exits
       without running the program. The load addresses are shown.

       For a shared library (no interpreter) ldd reads the ELF dynamic section
       itself and resolves each DT_NEEDED entry with the same search rules as
       the loader; no addresses are shown.

       A statically linked file produces "statically linked", a file that is
       not an ELF object produces "not a dynamic executable".

SEARCH ORDER
       1. DT_RPATH of the requesting object (only when it has no DT_RUNPATH)
       2. the directories in LD_LIBRARY_PATH
       3. DT_RUNPATH of the requesting object
       4. the cache /etc/ld.so.cache (see ldconfig(8))
       5. /lib, /usr/lib and /lib64

ENVIRONMENT
       LD_LIBRARY_PATH
           colon separated list of directories searched first.

       LD_TRACE_LOADED_OBJECTS
           when set to anything but 0 in the environment of a dynamic program,
           the loader lists the libraries and exits (this is what ldd sets).

       LD_DEBUG
           LD_DEBUG=libs makes the loader print every search attempt to
           standard error.

WARNING
       Because ldd starts the program under the loader, only use it on
       executables you trust.

SEE ALSO
       ldconfig(8)
